From 8ed168fe8366c7c9c01f21b45da5f56e055fedcf Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Bill Hlavacek <bill.hlavacek@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 5 Nov 2026 13:28:46 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] bngsim: reject a bond partner of the wrong molecule type
 before indexing it

TemplateMolecule::checkBonds and checkSymmetricComponents index a bond
partner's component arrays with an index that belongs to the PARTNER
TEMPLATE's molecule type, before anything has checked that the molecule is of
that type. Four sites:

  * checkBonds, normal branch: m2->getBondedMolecule(bondPartnerCompIndex[b])
    and getBondedMoleculeBindingSiteIndex(same).
  * checkBonds, symmetric branch: tryToMap() takes the equivalency-class
    component indices from t2's molecule type and applies them to m2
    (isBindingSiteOpen, getComponentState, getBondedMolecule).
  * checkSymmetricComponents, normal branch:
    m2->getBondedMolecule(symBondPartnerCompIndex[c]).
  * checkSymmetricComponents, symmetric branch: tryToMap() again.

The check exists -- compare() opens with a moleculeType comparison -- but it is
reached through t2->compare(m2,...) after those reads. Molecule::bond,
indexOfBond and component are sized to the molecule's own component count and
the accessors do not bounds-check, so a partner of a shorter type is read out
of bounds.

All four sites are reachable, and all four fault under AddressSanitizer:

  * checkBonds normal: tcr.bngl, where a ZAP70 whose bond partner template is
    LCP2 (4 components) is matched against a bond to a LAT (2 components) and
    read at index 3 of LAT's two-element array. The same mismatch
    also lands inside the array (index 1 on that LAT), reading an unrelated
    component's bond.
  * The other three each need a symmetric component off the reaction centre, and
    which side differs by site:
      - checkBonds' tryToMap branch: the PARTNER's side, which is what makes
        bondPartnerCompIndex negative and selects that branch.
      - checkSymmetricComponents' normal branch: the matched molecule's OWN
        side, with a concrete partner index.
      - checkSymmetricComponents' tryToMap branch: BOTH -- own-side symmetry to
        reach that function at all, since its loop runs over the matched
        template's own symmetric components, and partner-side to take the
        symmetric branch within it.
    On the reaction centre NFinput resolves a symmetric component to a concrete
    symPermutationName, so the symmetry has to sit elsewhere in the rule. That
    is why a sweep of 54 models reaches only the first site -- zero hits there
    is a property of the corpus, not evidence the others cannot happen.

Results are unaffected at every site -- the match is rejected a few lines later
either way -- but the read is undefined behaviour that faults when the array
ends at an unmapped page, which makes it an intermittent crash. It killed a
210-model network-free parity run with SIGSEGV after 185 minutes on tcr.bngl.

A molecule of another type cannot satisfy a template of this one, so the bond
cannot match, which is the verdict compare() reaches anyway. Each guard sits
above its branch dispatch, so it covers that function's symmetric tryToMap()
call as well as its normal-branch reads. checkBonds returns false, matching the
three early returns already in that branch; checkSymmetricComponents continues,
matching that loop's own idiom -- a return there would abandon the search over
the rest of the equivalency class, which origin/main does not do.

Over a 54-model corpus the checkBonds guard is entered 4,491,016 times and its
condition fires 36,375 times, with byte-identical output; the four hand-built
models that reach the other three sites are byte-identical too, and the
mismatch path is free (+5 instructions in the whole executable).

Upstream: RuleWorld/nfsim#113. BNGsim: GH #1543.
---
 src/NFcore/templateMolecule.cpp | 31 +++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 31 insertions(+)

diff --git a/src/NFcore/templateMolecule.cpp b/src/NFcore/templateMolecule.cpp
index bf6a69f..933a7c7 100644
--- a/src/NFcore/templateMolecule.cpp
+++ b/src/NFcore/templateMolecule.cpp
@@ -1097,6 +1097,25 @@ bool TemplateMolecule::checkBonds(Molecule *m, ReactantContainer *rc, MappingSet
 
 		//Now we have to make sure that the correct bond is present on the other side
 		//of the interaction. This can be tricky because of symmetric sites!
+		//Every index below -- bondPartnerCompIndex[b] in the normal branch, and
+		//the equivalency-class indices tryToMap() takes from t2's type in the
+		//symmetric one -- is a component index in the PARTNER TEMPLATE's molecule
+		//type, applied to m2. That only means anything if m2 is of that type, and
+		//nothing has checked yet: the check is in t2->compare(m2,...), reached
+		//after those reads. Molecule::bond and Molecule::indexOfBond are sized to
+		//the molecule's own component count and the accessors do not bounds-check,
+		//so a molecule of a shorter type is read out of bounds -- in tcr.bngl a
+		//ZAP70 bound to a LAT (2 components) is read at LCP2's component index 3,
+		//past the end of LAT's two-element bond array, which AddressSanitizer
+		//reports as a heap-buffer-overflow here. Where the stray index lands
+		//inside the array instead, it reads an unrelated component's bond.
+		//A molecule of another type cannot satisfy a template of this one, so the
+		//bond cannot match -- which is the verdict compare() reaches anyway. Answer
+		//it before reading anything.
+		if(m2->getMoleculeType()!=t2->getMoleculeType()) {
+			return false;
+		}
+
 		if(bondPartnerCompIndex[b]<0) { //Argh!  this means we are symmetric!!
 
 
@@ -1204,6 +1223,18 @@ bool TemplateMolecule::checkSymmetricComponents(Molecule *m, ReactantContainer *
 				}
 
 
+				//Same unchecked partner-template index as in checkBonds above:
+				//symBondPartnerCompIndex[c] and tryToMap()'s equivalency-class
+				//indices both belong to t2's molecule type and are applied to m2,
+				//and the only type check is in the t2->compare(m2,...) reached
+				//after them. A molecule of another type cannot match, so skip this
+				//candidate before reading it -- `continue` rather than `return
+				//false` because this loop is searching the symmetric components
+				//for a mapping, and every path under a type mismatch continues.
+				if(m2->getMoleculeType()!=t2->getMoleculeType()) {
+					continue;
+				}
+
 				if(symBondPartnerCompIndex[c]<0) { //Argh!  this means we are symmetric!!
 					//See if we can map this bond to one of the symmetric components on m2
 					bool canMap = t2->tryToMap(m2,symBondPartnerCompName[c],m,this->symCompName[c]);
